57
00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:25.830
Pasquale Convertini: If you look at the releases, you may have seen that we have moved from 03 to 04, and so here, I'm gonna quickly explain the reasons, the main changes between 03 and 0.4.

58
00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.140
Pasquale Convertini: for the Ansible collection.

59
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:36.220
Pasquale Convertini: So, as of today, we have…

60
00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:44.379
Pasquale Convertini: the version 047. You can find it on GitHub or on Ansible Galaxy.

61
00:09:44.710 --> 00:10:02.869
Pasquale Convertini: And, so, if we look at what's changed in, between 03 and 04, basically, there are two main, topics to, to discuss. One is the crypto material generation.

62
00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.789
Pasquale Convertini: So, we are transitioning from

63
00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:24.989
Pasquale Convertini: from a deployment logic, where the crypto material was initially generated on the control node, and then distributed to the remote node, to, logic, thanks also to the introduction of Fabric CA,

64
00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:42.599
Pasquale Convertini: To the generation of the crypto material directly on the remote node, which of course gives better security standards, because the crypto, especially the private key, is generated directly on the machine where the component is supposed to run.

65
00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:47.639
Pasquale Convertini: So it's not transferred between the control node and the remote node.

66
00:10:48.200 --> 00:11:04.500
Pasquale Convertini: On the contrary, what we do, we actually do now the opposite. So instead of transferring from the control node to the remote node, the private key and the public key, we fetch now from the remote node only the public key.

67
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:14.130
Pasquale Convertini: or, let's say, TLS certificates, which are then needed to build other artifacts. For instance, the Genesis block.

68
00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:26.500
Pasquale Convertini: We have then… so this fetching step, is what also is uniformed the flow, that we can run.

69
00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:46.169
Pasquale Convertini: Either with Cryptogen or FabricCA. If you look inside the collection, you will find some, some inventory examples, so one is FabricX, which by default uses FabricCA, but there is another one which is called FabricX Cryptogen, which uses Cryptogen under the hood.

70
00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:57.160
Pasquale Convertini: And, you may need to use this one if you don't go in production, let's say you want to do a quick test with Cryptogen, it's much faster to generate the crypto material.

71
00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:11.179
Pasquale Convertini: But there was a problem then how to uniform the rest of the deployment when, you know, use Cryptogen instead of FabricCA. Now, with this fetch mechanism, basically, we are able to merge

72
00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:16.760
Pasquale Convertini: the flows, and there is no more discrepancy between FabricCA and CryptoGen.

73
00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:26.589
Pasquale Convertini: Then, we have started, the support for TLS on…

74
00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:33.480
Pasquale Convertini: almost all the components that can support TLS in the deployment.

75
00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:50.789
Pasquale Convertini: To do so, we have improved the role OpenSSL, which is used to generate TLS certificates for the components that don't rely on Fabric CA. Otherwise, Fabric CA already gives support to generate TLS cryptomaterial.

76
00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:57.399
Pasquale Convertini: And then, we have added the TLS support for the following components, so Postgres, YugoByte.

77
00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.330
Pasquale Convertini: And then a bunch of… other,

78
00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:07.320
