81
00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:35.110
Pasquale Convertini: We're used to the commands of 0.3. 0.4 doesn't introduce any breaking change. You do make setup, make start to generate the artifacts and start the FabricX network.

82
00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:48.419
Pasquale Convertini: How to enable TLS. So, the logic is the following. Each component can enable TLS by adding in the inventory the flag.

83
00:13:48.550 --> 00:14:00.479
Pasquale Convertini: of the component name, underscore use, underscore TLS to true. So here you see an example for the monitoring infrastructure, where we enable TLS for Node Exporter, or

84
00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:22.200
Pasquale Convertini: the Postgres exporter, Prometus and Grafana, the logic is always the same. For each component, you specify the flag, and you put it to true. And then, Ansible will take care of everything else. So we'll generate the TLS certificates, put them in the right position, and then distribute among all the components when needed.

85
00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:41.859
Pasquale Convertini: quick, quick, quick update on what's going… what's coming in the… in the next weeks. Support for TLS and MTLS for the… for these components, the order, the committer, and the FX config.

86
00:14:42.650 --> 00:15:07.050
Pasquale Convertini: Which will bring us to claim a full TLS deployment of Fabric X. And yeah, if you want to be up to date, I highly recommend to always look at the official repository, or directly check the releases, because for each release, we usually define what has been added or what has been changed.

